e band immediately below, 5.6 contracts would be expected. The observed-to-expected ratio is 3.9, with a 95% confidence interval of 2.1 to 7.1. December accounts for 0% of the contracts just under the limit, compared with 0% of all its works contracts. The awardee with the most contracts just under the limit, called awardee A here, has 3. Contract [1], for 39,801.62 euros, was awarded in November 2025.\nWhat it cannot show: The data shows that some contracts were just under the limit, but it doesn't prove any wrongdoing. Prices might be lower than expected, or budgets might be fixed. The numbers alone don't show if rules were broken or if anyone acted improperly.\nWhat can be checked: Check the details of contract [1] to see what was purchased and the dates. Look at awardee A's contracts to see if they were awarded for the same need. Verify if multiple contracts were used for a single requirement to ensure compliance with procurement rules.","origen_es":"modelo","origen_en":"modelo","modelo":"Qwen3-4B (Q4_K_M, llama.cpp)","regla":"obras","citas":["ikDu33PMgq%2FVGIpKDxgsAQ%3D%3D","S7rTTkMSvImKeVWTb9Scog%3D%3D","neQl%2B50n3NJeKgd8LfVV9g%3D%3D"],"generado":"2026-11-16"}}